# Development API base URL for the mobile app.
#
# `localhost` is correct for the iOS simulator, which shares the host's network
# stack, and for Android — but Android needs help: inside an emulator (and on a
# USB-attached phone) `localhost` means the device itself, not this Mac. The
# bridge is `adb reverse tcp:8080 tcp:8080`, applied by `make mb-reverse` once
# the device or emulator is up. (`make dev` opens iOS only; Android is manual.)
#
# The one case this value does NOT cover is a physical iPhone: there is no adb,
# so it must reach the Mac over the network. Point this at the machine's LAN IP
# (`ipconfig getifaddr en0`) for that session — the API already binds
# 0.0.0.0:8080, so it is reachable. The healthcheck screen prints whatever
# resolves here, so a wrong value is visible on first launch.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Brandfetch LOGO LINK CLIENT ID — brand logos in the garage and the catalog.
#
# THIS IS THE PUBLIC ONE, AND IT IS NOT THE BRAND API KEY. Brandfetch issues
# two different credentials and only one of them may ever be in this file:
#
#   Logo Link  client id   PUBLIC   cdn.brandfetch.io/{domain}?c={id}
#                                   Designed to sit in an <img> URL. It goes
#                                   here. It looks like "1idAbCdEf...".
#   Brand API  API key     SECRET   Authorization: Bearer {key}
#                                   NEVER here. EXPO_PUBLIC_* is compiled into
#                                   the bundle, so anyone who unzips the IPA
#                                   reads it, and a leaked bearer token cannot
#                                   be un-leaked. That one belongs in the root
#                                   .env, behind the API, and needs server code
#                                   that does not exist yet.
#
# Get the Logo Link id at https://developers.brandfetch.com — free tier.
#
# LEFT EMPTY HERE ON PURPOSE. This file is tracked and the repository is
# public; the id is not a secret, but its free-tier quota is attached to it and
# a public repo is a scraping target. Put the real value in
# .env.development.local, which .gitignore already covers and which both Expo
# and the Makefile load after this file so it wins:
#
#     echo "EXPO_PUBLIC_BRANDFETCH_CLIENT_ID=1id..." > apps/mobile/.env.development.local
#
# Leaving it empty is still a supported state, not a broken one: the app falls
# back to a 48x48 favicon, which is the ceiling every keyless source serves.
# See src/features/catalog/brandLogo.ts.
EXPO_PUBLIC_BRANDFETCH_CLIENT_ID=
